To avoid this error, you can use the `get` method with the optional chaining operator (`?.`) to safely access the inner Map and the budget object, and use a default value if either of them are `undefined`.
Here is an example of how you could update the code to avoid the runtime error:
```
if (equipment.approval === ApprovalStatus.APPROVED) {
  const yearBudgets = budgets.get(year) || new Map<string, {
    [EquipmentType.OFFICE]: number;
    [EquipmentType.PERSONAL]: number;
  }>();
  const userBudget = yearBudgets.get(equipment.userId) || {
    [EquipmentType.OFFICE]: 0,
    [EquipmentType.PERSONAL]: 0,
  };
  userBudget[equipment.type] += equipment.cost;
  yearBudgets.set(equipment.userId, userBudget);
  budgets.set(year, yearBudgets
```

